# it requires that the first previous block is defined before the loop, in the form of a Block object which
# can be obtained by parsing a raw block bytes. This is if you have the raw block bytes of the previous block.
# You can get the raw block bytes from a block explorer website.
# In this example, I requeste blocks 3, 4, and 5, as I sued their hashed Id as start_block
# # and stop_block. this gives me the first header of  block 4, and then block 5. But I am tricking it by 
# saying  use block 4 as the previous block, (sinc I dont want to go get the previous block's raw btye and 
# give it to "Block.pars()" to give me a block object so that the fileds like previou.timestam and previous.prev_hash and ...
# to work, this way I use the first header that comes in as my first blcok object.. in short 
# I sacrifise the first block. So, instead of getting headers 4 and 5 we use 4 as or first block object
# and then we only get 5. 
# So in the for loop, I skip the first header by using headers.blocks[1:5] instead of headers.blocks
# This is just for demonstration purposes. so that I dont have to go get the raw bytes of block 3
# and parse it to get a block object for previous. 
# In real application, you should get the previous block's raw bytes and parse it to get a block object
# and use that as previous before the for loop, so that you can process all the headers received.
